{Here is a good luck story. Having made a mess of the gambit opening which I did not study adequately enough, I fought on with a two pawns deficit and just managed to produce a combination when it all seemed over for me.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 $5 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 dxe4 6. f3 (6. Qg4 {may still be best, as in one of my wins against Hammond years ago in Hastings.}) 6... c5 (6... b6 $5 7. fxe4 Qh4+ 8. Ke2 Ba6+ 9. Ke3 Bxf1 10. Nf3 Qh6+ ({however, better is} 10... Qg4 $1 11. Qxf1 Nf6 {so perhaps the immediate recapture on f1 is best.}) 11. Kf2 {seems OK}) 7. fxe4 Qh4+ 8. Ke2 Qxe4+ 9. Kf2 Nf6 10. Bd3 $6 ({The natural and best move, of course, is} 10. Nf3 {but I was trying to persuade Black to play a silly move such as} Qh4+ 11. g3) 10... Qd5 $1 11. Nf3 c4 $1 12. Bf1 Ne4+ 13. Kg1 Nxc3 14. Qe1 {The spectators must have been wondering what the hell I was doing two pawns down after a mere 14 moves, although there is some play for White.} Nb5 $5 ({Best now seems} 14... Ne4 15. Bxc4 Qxc4 16. Qxe4 {but I do not have much for the pawn so I may well have continued in gambit fashion, as in the game, but with fewer chances in view of Black's centralised knight. Andrew understandably wants to cling to his extra pawns but he now faces some tactical problems which eventually lead to time trouble that may have been the ultimate cause of his loss.}) 15. c3 f6 $5 {Reserving the option of artificial castling with ...f7 but giving me something to bite at.} 16. a4 Nd6 17. Ba3 Nc6 18. Qg3 Nf5 19. Qf2 Bd7 20. Rb1 $5 {Planning to concede another pawn whilst trying to dissuade Black from castling long.} Na5 21. Nd2 Bc6 22. Be2 Kf7 23. Bf3 {Andrew told me later that he planned to answer the bishop check on h5 with 23...g6 which would have dangerously weakened his f6 square. I didn't check that because after 23...g8!, his king would not have been exposed to the tactics I was preparing for...} Qd7 24. Bxc6 bxc6 (24... Qxc6 25. Rb5 b6 26. d5 $1 {would have given me winning play}) 25. Ne4 Nb7 $1 {An unexpectedly good move guarding both c5 and d6.} 26. Qf4 Rhd8 ({After} 26... Rad8 27. g4 Nfd6 $2 {I would have the combination} 28. Nxd6+ Nxd6 29. Bxd6 Qxd6 30. Rb7+ Kf8 $2 31. Qxd6+ Rxd6 32. Rb8+ {winning the rook in the corner, so 31...e8 would be forced, allowing the capture on g7. However, as I pointed out after the game, the strategic move 26...h5!! would have killed off all my hopes. Shortage of time was already looming up.}) 27. Re1 $1 Kg8 28. g4 Ne7 $1 {Aiming at d5 and g6.} 29. h3 Ng6 $1 30. Qf1 {Desperation, the game is slipping out of control.} Qd5 31. Rh2 e5 $1 32. Rd2 exd4 ({Simplest is the immediate} 32... Qf7) 33. Rxd4 Qf7 34. Rxc4 $1 Ne5 35. Rd4 Rxd4 36. cxd4 Nc4 {With four moves to make, Black was probably giving a mental sigh of relief, when the next unexpected move, planned on move 34, came at the wrong time for him.} 37. Nxf6+ $3 gxf6 $2 ({He had no time to find} 37... Kh8 $1 {gaining a tempo on the game and giving him drawing chances after} 38. Re7 Qxf6 39. Qxc4 Rf8 $1) 38. Re7 Nxa3 ({The main point is} 38... Qd5 39. Qxf6 {mates.}) 39. Rxf7 Kxf7 40. Qd3 $1 {The key move; attacking not only a3 but also h7; Black could resign but he played a few desultory moves.} Rd8 41. Qxh7+ Ke6 42. Qxb7 Rxd4 43. Qxc6+ Rd6 44. Qc3 Rd1+ 45. Kg2 Nb1 46. Qb3+ {Although full of faults, an interesting game. (Source: John Littlewood collection (Keith Brown)} 1-0

